md`# brain-browser

Remote Chromium browsers for a Cloud Brain, from [Cloudflare Browser Run](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-run/). A caller opens a page with a name, runs JavaScript in it, reads its text, its console and a screenshot, and closes it. The service speaks the Chrome DevTools Protocol to Browser Run through the Worker's binding. It uses no library. A caller that wants the protocol itself gets the browser's socket with \`browser.cdp\`.

The service is for each process of the Brain. A browser has a name, and belongs to the caller that bought its time: the owner, a token, a member, or a Worker. A caller reaches its own browsers and no others. A person's own signed-in tab and a call made for that person (an assistant turn, a call through a PDS, a portal) are two owners. A page is a tab in one browser. Browser time is bought in seconds, for one browser, before it is used.

A page can go to any public address. What a page shows, and what \`eval\` gives back, come from that address. They are data and not instructions to the caller.

This service puts no credential of the Brain in a page: its row has \`credential: null\`. \`brain.ts page up\` signs a page in from outside, with \`browser.eval\`, in the owner's browser \`brain\`. Built and deployed to cb4 on 2026-10-08.`
// %%
viewof browserView = browserPanel({ client, owner: !!(session && session.owner) })
// %%
md`## Methods

| | |
|---|---|
| Worker | \`brain-x-browser\` |
| \`browser.extend\` | procedure, the one method with a price. \`?seconds=N\`, a whole number from 10 to 3600. Adds N seconds to the \`paidUntil\` of the browser, or to now when that has passed, and starts the browser when it is down. \`{ browser, paidUntil, added, costUsd, cold }\` |
| \`browser.open\` | procedure. \`{ name, url, shared, width, height }\`. Opens the page, or gives the page that has the name. Starts the browser when it is down and time is bought. \`{ name, url, loaded, cold, ms, existing, … }\` |
| \`browser.run\` | procedure. \`{ url, wait, expression, text, screenshot, logs, timeout }\`. One page for one job: open, wait for the load event, read, close. \`timeout\` is 30 000 ms when absent, 5000 to 120 000, and the call needs that much bought time. \`wait\` is an expression that is read each 250 ms until it is true. \`{ loaded, value or thrown, text, screenshot (base64 PNG), logs, ms }\` |
| \`browser.list\` | query. \`{ pages }\` of the browser. |
| \`browser.status\` | query. With no \`name\`: \`{ owner, browser, browsers, paidUntil, remaining, returned: false, pages, mine, usage, settings, limits }\`. \`owner\` is who owns the browser: the caller, or \`via:<caller>\`. \`mine\` is each browser of that owner: \`{ owner, browser, paidUntil, remaining, up, pages }\`. With \`?name=\`: the page, or \`{ open: false, closed, at }\` for a page that closed in the last 24 h. |
| \`browser.close\` | procedure. \`{ name }\` closes the page and leaves the browser. \`{ all: true }\` closes the browser at once; the time stays bought. |
| \`browser.goto\` | procedure. \`{ name, url }\`. Loads a different address in the same tab. |
| \`browser.eval\` | procedure. \`{ name, expression, awaitPromise, timeout }\`. \`{ value, type }\`, or \`{ thrown: { message, line, column } }\` with status 200 when the page throws. A promise is awaited unless \`awaitPromise\` is \`false\`. \`timeout\` is 20 000 ms when absent, 120 000 at most. |
| \`browser.text\` | query. \`?name=&selector=\`. \`{ text, truncated }\`: the \`innerText\` of the body or of the first match, 100 000 characters at most. |
| \`browser.screenshot\` | query. \`?name=&fullPage=true&selector=\`. PNG bytes, 2 000 000 at most. |
| \`browser.logs\` | query. \`?name=\`. \`{ logs: [{ t, level, text }] }\`: console calls and uncaught errors of the open document, the last 200, 500 characters each. |
| \`browser.cdp\` | a WebSocket: a GET with \`Upgrade: websocket\`. \`?browser=\`. The browser's own Chrome DevTools Protocol socket. Starts the browser when it is down and time is bought. A GET that is not an upgrade answers 426 \`UpgradeRequired\`. |
| \`browser.liveView\` | query, the owner's own session. \`?name=&browser=\`. \`{ url }\`: a Browser Run Live View link to the tab. |
| \`browser.all\` | query, the owner's own session. \`{ browsers: [{ owner, browser, paidUntil, remaining, up, pages }] }\`: each browser of each owner. |
| \`browser.end\` | procedure, the owner's own session. \`{ owner, browser }\`, as \`all\` gives them. Closes that browser at once. \`{ closed, browser }\`, where \`closed\` is the pages that were open: it is \`[]\` also when the owner or the browser does not exist. |
| \`browser.settings\` | procedure, the owner's own session. \`{ maxTabs, maxBrowsers, maxPerOwner }\`. |
| Storage | \`rows\`. For one browser, under \`b/<owner>/<browser>/\`: \`browser/<session>\`, \`paid\`, \`page/<name>\`, \`ended/<name>\`, \`starting/shared\`. For the service: \`usage/<day>\`, \`settings\`. |

Each method of the first twelve, and \`liveView\`, takes \`browser\`, a name of 1 to 40 letters, digits, \`-\` and \`_\` that starts with a letter or a digit, in the query string or in the body. It is \`default\` when absent. \`extend\` reads it from the query string only.

\`\`\`js
await client.procedure("browser.extend", {}, { params: { seconds: 60 } })
// { browser: "default", paidUntil: 1791450000000, added: 60, costUsd: 0.0015, cold: true }
await client.procedure("browser.run", { url: "https://example.com/", expression: "document.title" })
// { url, loaded: true, cold: false, value: "Example Domain", type: "string", ms: 665 }
// A second browser of the same caller, with its own time and its own tabs:
await client.procedure("browser.extend", {}, { params: { browser: "scrape", seconds: 60 } })
await client.procedure("browser.run", { browser: "scrape", url: "https://example.com/" })
\`\`\`

Each method but \`extend\`, \`list\`, \`status\`, \`close\`, \`all\`, \`end\` and \`settings\` needs bought time in its browser. With none it answers 409 \`NoTime\` with \`paidUntil\`, and the message names the browser and \`browser.extend\`.

\`seconds\` is in the query string and not in the body, because a price reads \`request.params\` and does not read the body.

Limits. \`eval\` gives a value that JSON can hold, 262 144 characters at most. The viewport on cb4 was 780 × 493: the \`width\` and \`height\` that \`open\` asks for were not applied by Browser Run. \`logs\` starts again with each document, and a page that loads a new document by itself has no log from before its first script until the next call. A Live View link gives control of the tab to the person who holds it; how long the link works was not measured.`
// %%
md`## The CDP socket

\`browser.cdp\` gives the caller the WebSocket that Browser Run gives this Worker for the browser. The service asks Browser Run for the upgrade and returns its answer. It reads no frame, changes none and holds no connection. Thus each CDP command and each CDP event is there, and a client made for CDP connects with no code of ours.

\`\`\`js
// Raw. A server-side WebSocket can send the header; bun and node (ws) do.
const ws = new WebSocket("wss://BRAIN/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.browser.cdp", { headers: { authorization: "Bearer " + token } });
ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data); // answers by id, and events
ws.onopen = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id: 1, method: "Target.createTarget", params: { url: "https://example.com/" } }));

// playwright-core, as it is:
const browser = await chromium.connectOverCDP("wss://BRAIN/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.browser.cdp", { headers: { authorization: "Bearer " + token } });

// A service of the Brain, by its own key (it declares the method in calls):
const r = await xrpc.fetch("com.lopecode.brain.browser.cdp", { headers: { Upgrade: "websocket" } });
r.webSocket.accept();
\`\`\`

Use the socket for what needs events or real input: a click or a key that the page sees as trusted, a wait for a navigation, the network of a page, a download. Use the methods above for one job with one answer; they need no open connection and each is one line in the metrics.

The socket is the whole browser, so it reaches each tab in it, also the tabs that \`open\` made. That is the caller's own browser and no other. A tab that the socket makes has no row: \`list\` does not show it and the limit of pages does not count it.

Limits.
- The socket closes when the browser ends. A browser ends at the tick after its bought time has passed, so the socket can live up to 60 s past \`paidUntil\`; the close has code 1005 and no reason. \`extend\` on the same browser while the socket is open moves the end.
- The rule and \`calls\` are decided one time, at the upgrade. The metrics have one row for the socket, with the time to the 101 and not the time it was open.
- A browser page cannot set the \`Authorization\` header of a WebSocket, and the kernel reads no cookie. A page of the Brain has no way to open this socket today.
- A deploy under an open socket was tried one time, on a scratch service: the socket went on for 172 s more, until the client closed it. Cloudflare says an in-flight request gets 30 s at a runtime update. A deploy of the core or the kernel was not tried. A client must be able to connect again.

Measured on cb4, 2026-10-08 18:14 to 18:17 UTC, client in Berlin. Scripts in \`tools/scratch/ws-probe\`; the record is \`tools/cloud-brain/websockets.md\`.

\`\`\`
raw socket      connect 768 ms (browser just started)   Runtime.evaluate p50 60.7 ms, n=40
browser.eval    p50 427 ms, n=12                         the same expression, one call each
playwright-core connectOverCDP 1020 ms; goto example.com 196 ms; click the link and wait for iana.org 1629 ms
                keyboard.type("lope") -> 4 keydown events, each isTrusted; 4 request events; a PNG of 5137 bytes
brain-x-wscdp   by its own key: 101 through the core, Browser.getVersion "Chrome/128.0.6613.137",
                events Target.targetCreated, Target.targetInfoChanged; its browser was worker:brain-x-wscdp/default
bought 20 s, +30 s by extend while connected -> the socket closed 13.3 s after the new paidUntil
\`\`\`
`
// %%
md`## Many browsers, each with tabs

A browser is the pair of its owner and its name. The owner is the caller that the core names (\`x-brain-caller\`): \`owner\`, \`token:NAME\`, a member's DID, or \`worker:brain-x-NAME\`. The service does not read an owner from a parameter, thus a caller cannot name the browser of a different caller. A member's Worker calls as its author, so the two share browsers.

How the caller arrived also counts (\`x-brain-via\`). A call with \`turn:<entry>\`, \`jwt\` or \`portal:<id>\` is not the person's own tab, and its owner is \`via:<caller>\`: rows under \`b/via:<caller>/\`. All turns, PDS calls and portals of one caller share that owner. A call under a delegation (\`delegation:<id>\`) has the owner \`d:<id>\`, apart from the person's and from every other delegation's. A person's own session, a token and a Worker keep the caller's name. The reason is that a page in a person's browser can hold that person's session: \`brain.ts page up\` puts the owner's in the browser \`brain\`, and a turn of the owner has the caller \`owner\`. Until 2026-10-09 the turn reached that page with \`eval\`. \`maxPerOwner\` counts the two owners apart, so a turn cannot use up the browsers of the session. \`all\` lists both and \`end\` closes either; \`liveView\` reaches the session's own only. Each browser is one Browser Run session: its own memory, its own \`paidUntil\`, its own end.

Who owns and who pays are different. A Worker that calls in a chain the owner started owns the browser, and the core charges the owner, who is the origin of the chain. Until 2026-10-08 19:38 CEST the service had one browser for all callers; it changed because "each browser will be owned by another process, so this one thing has to serve the whole cluster" (Tom, 2026-10-08).

\`open\` makes a tab in the browser. Each page has its own browser context, thus two pages on one origin do not share cookies, \`localStorage\` or IndexedDB. Two pages opened with the same \`shared: "label"\` share one context.

Each call but \`cdp\` makes a new WebSocket to the browser, attaches to the tab by its target id, and closes the socket. No Durable Object holds a connection. Thus a page keeps its state between calls, and two calls at one time each have a connection.

The tabs of one browser share its fate. If the browser ends, each of its pages is closed, and the next call answers \`PageClosed\` with the reason: \`browser ended\`, \`time ran out\`, \`page ended\`, \`browser stopped answering\`. They also share its memory. Cloudflare does not publish the limit. Three tabs of the Brain's own page in one browser stopped it on cb4 two times in two attempts (see Measured), and two tabs stopped it one time in two. One page of the Brain for each browser is the safe number.

| Setting | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| \`maxTabs\` | 8 | pages in one browser |
| \`maxBrowsers\` | 10 | browsers up at one time, of all owners |
| \`maxPerOwner\` | 3 | browsers up at one time that one caller owns |

Cloudflare allows 200 browsers at one time for an account on Workers Paid, 3 on Workers Free, and 3 new browsers a second ([limits](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-rendering/limits/), read 2026-10-08). Over a limit of this service, the call that would start a browser answers 429 \`TooManyBrowsers\` and names the setting. The count is read and then the browser is started, so two starts at one moment can pass a limit by one.

Two calls that start one browser at one time: the row \`starting/shared\` of that browser is a lock, and the call that does not get it waits for the browser row. This service closes only the sessions it has a row for, because the list of sessions is for the Cloudflare account and holds the sessions of other Workers. If a start dies after Browser Run made the browser and before the row, that browser gets no command, and Browser Run's idle limit (180 s, at most $0.0045) closes it. That close was not measured.

A cron tick each minute goes through the browsers that are up. It closes one when its time ran out, or sends it one command (Browser Run closes a browser that gets no command for 180 s), and drops the rows of a browser or a tab that is gone. The rows of a browser that is over are deleted 24 h later.`
// %%
md`## Who can call

The core decides who can call a method. This service does not decide. It keeps the rows of each caller apart, and records who started the chain of calls in \`origin\`.

The rule of the first twelve methods is \`caller.trusted || caller.kind in ["worker", "deployer"]\` (\`who: "workers"\`): the owner, a token that names the method, an account with a grant, and a Worker of this Brain. A recipe Worker must also name the method in its \`calls\`. A member's Worker calls with its author's grant and no more. The owner can put a different rule with \`rule.put\`. A call with no caller has no owner and gets 401 \`AuthRequired\` from this service, whatever the rule: a rule that lets everyone in would give all of them one browser.

\`settings\`, \`liveView\`, \`all\` and \`end\` are \`caller.session\`, the owner's own session, and the owner cannot put a different rule for them. \`all\` and \`end\` are the only methods that reach the browser of a different caller.

Addresses. \`open\`, \`goto\` and \`run\` refuse an address that is not \`http:\` or \`https:\`, \`localhost\`, a name that ends in \`.localhost\`, \`.internal\` or \`.local\`, and a private or link-local IP address. Only the address in the call is read. A redirect, and what the page loads by itself, are not checked. \`browser.cdp\` checks no address: its caller sends the browser's own commands and can go to any address, \`file:\`, \`chrome:\` and a private IP address included. The caller owns that browser. The browser runs in Cloudflare's network and not in the Brain's.

A caller that can run JavaScript in a signed-in page can do what the page can do. \`brain.ts page up\` signs a page in with the owner's session, in a browser that the owner's own session owns. A turn, a PDS call and a portal of the owner do not reach it (see **Many browsers, each with tabs**). A token and a Worker have browsers of their own (\`b/token:NAME/\`, \`b/worker:NAME/\`) and do not reach it either.

The assistant has no tool for this service. A page reads text from any address, and that text would come into the assistant's turn.`
// %%
md`## Time and cost

Cloudflare bills Browser Run by the browser-hour: $0.09 an hour, 10 hours a month in the Workers Paid plan, and the total of a month is rounded to the hour ([pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-rendering/pricing/), read 2026-10-07). One second is $0.000025. The count is for the account and not for this service.

Browser time is bought before it is used, by one method, for one browser. \`extend?browser=NAME&seconds=N\` adds N seconds to the \`paidUntil\` of that browser, or to now when it has passed. Its price is N × 0.000025, and no other method has a price. The core charges that to the account of who started the call before the call reaches this service (see **Prices and credits** in \`@tomlarkworthy/brain-core\`), and refuses the call with 402 \`OutOfCredits\` when the account's day is spent.

What is charged is what is added. The price is 0 for a \`seconds\` that this service refuses (absent, not digits, less than 10, more than 3600), and the service then answers 400. A purchase is one appended entry in the row \`paid\`, so purchases made at one time all count. The row keeps the last 1000 purchases. Each entry carries the time that was bought before it, so the oldest can go.

Time that is bought is not given back: not when a page is closed, not on \`close { all: true }\` or \`end\`, and not when an \`extend\` starts no browser, because Browser Run failed or because a limit of browsers was reached (429 \`TooManyBrowsers\`). In those cases the seconds stay bought for that browser, and the next call that needs it starts it when it can. The time counts from the purchase whether or not a browser started: an hour bought at a limit that does not clear in the hour buys nothing. The core charges before this service runs, and a price cannot read how many browsers are up. The most one \`extend\` can cost is 3600 × $0.000025 = $0.09.

When \`paidUntil\` has passed, the next call or the next tick closes the browser, whatever tabs are open. The use of a page does not add time. A browser with time left and no page stays up. The tick comes each minute, thus a browser can live up to 60 s after \`paidUntil\` when no call comes.

This service has no spending limit of its own. Until 2026-10-08 07:27 CEST it had a daily limit of seconds and a ledger; the core's price took their place, so that one mechanism counts money. Until 2026-10-08, \`open\` and \`run\` also bought time and the core gave back what was not bought; that was removed for less code (Tom, 2026-10-08).

\`status\` gives \`usage\`: the browser-seconds that the browsers of all owners ran today and this month, as the tick counted them, to the minute. The charges are in the core (\`quota.ledger\`). Browser Run's own record (\`/v1/history\` on the binding) has the exact start and end of each session; this service does not read it.`
// %%
md`## Measured

On cb4, 2026-10-08, 04:53 to 05:24 UTC, HeadlessChrome 128. Browser Run's history gave 411.8 browser-seconds for the day's 10 sessions, the probes included.

\`\`\`
run  https://example.com/  cold browser  1831 ms   warm browser  665 ms, 1075 ms
open https://example.com/  cold browser  2036 ms   warm browser  1100 ms, 1134 ms
open the Brain's page      cold browser  2185 ms, 2408 ms   warm browser  1238, 1261, 1076 ms
\`\`\`

\`open\` and \`run\` answer at the load event. The Brain's page was ready (the runtime up, "Sign in" on the page) 5.5 to 6.0 s after navigation, with about 5000 variables.

JavaScript heap of the Brain's page, not signed in, from \`performance.memory\` in each of two tabs:

\`\`\`
+11 s  151 MB  180 MB
+23 s  472 MB  338 MB
+35 s  397 MB  399 MB
\`\`\`

Three tabs of that page: 63 to 77 MB each at +5.5 s. In both attempts the browser stopped answering 13 to 21 s after the third tab opened, and Browser Run closed the session with \`WorkerError\` 92 s and 115 s after its start. Three tabs of example.com held for 30 s with no fault. The cause was not isolated. Memory is the likely one: two tabs reached about 800 MB of heap.

Held on the binding, with a scratch Worker: \`Target.createBrowserContext\` works, and two contexts on one origin did not see each other's \`localStorage\`. Two Worker requests held a connection to one session at one time. \`DELETE /v1/devtools/browser/<id>\` closes a session. \`GET /v1/sessions\` lists the sessions of the account with no owner.

A fault that was found and designed out: with \`Runtime.evaluate\` left waiting on a promise that did not settle, the request's client gave up after 30 s, each later call answered \`BrowserUnavailable\`, and \`close\` did not close the session. \`eval\` now leaves no command waiting: the promise writes its result to a place in the page, and that place is read each 100 to 500 ms. After the change, three calls that waited on promises at one time answered. A probe of the same case with a second request ended in a Worker error; that probe was not repeated and its cause is not known.

Many browsers, on cb4, 2026-10-08 17:38 to 17:42 UTC, as the owner's session and as the scratch Worker \`brain-x-bruser\` (removed after):

\`\`\`
extend?browser=a&seconds=60   cold: true      extend?browser=b&seconds=60   cold: true
open a/one  762 ms            open b/one  784 ms     two sessions, a tab named "one" in each
brain-x-bruser by its key: extend 20 s, run example.com 711 ms -> "Example Domain"
  its status: owner "worker:brain-x-bruser", browser "default"; the charge went to the owner, who started the chain
  list?owner=owner&browser=a -> { pages: [] } (its own browser "a")   eval of the owner's page -> 409 NoTime
  browser.all 401   browser.end 401   (the rule; the owner's own session only)
owner's browser.all: owner/a, owner/b, worker:brain-x-bruser/default, all up
close { browser: "a", all: true } -> eval in b: "Example Domain"; eval in a: 410 PageClosed
\`\`\`

Three tabs of the Brain's page in two browsers, two in \`a\` and one in \`b\`: at +35 s the heaps were 504 and 482 MB in \`a\` and 556 MB in \`b\`. Between +35 s and +60 s browser \`a\` ended (\`connect answered 410\`), and \`b\` still answered (491 MB). One browser's end did not touch the other. Two tabs of that page in one browser held on the morning of 2026-10-08 and did not hold here, thus one Brain page for each browser is the safe number. The round used 340 bought seconds, $0.0085.

Measured on 2026-10-07 with the spike in \`tools/scratch/browser-run\`: a session lived 150 s with no connection and was reattached by its id; one tab of the Brain's page held for 11 min.`
// %%
md`## Not built

Hosting the Brain's own page in a browser. It needs an identity for the page (a session that is not the owner's own), and the proxy as the page's path to a model. The page gets a browser of its own, by a name.

Also not built: a limit on the hosts a page can load (Browser Run has \`guardrails.allowedDomains\`, set when a session starts, thus for a browser and not for a tab); a viewport size; methods for a click, a key or a download (\`browser.cdp\` carries them); the close of a tab that has no row (a call that dies between making the tab and writing its row leaves the tab until the browser ends); a read of \`/v1/history\` for the exact seconds.`
// %%
browser_service = cloudflare.Service("browser", browserApp, {
  crons: ["* * * * *"],
  scheduled: () => browserOps.tick(),
  methods: {
    // Time is bought by extend alone, at Browser Run's $0.09 an hour. The price is 0 for a number the service refuses, so a refused call costs nothing.
    // who: "workers" is the owner, a token, a granted account, and a Worker of this Brain. A caller reaches the browsers it owns and no others.
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.extend": { type: "procedure", who: "workers", price: browserPrice },
    ...Object.fromEntries(["open", "run", "close", "goto", "eval"].map((m) => ["com.lopecode.brain.browser." + m, { type: "procedure", who: "workers" }])),
    ...Object.fromEntries(["list", "status", "text", "screenshot", "logs", "cdp"].map((m) => ["com.lopecode.brain.browser." + m, { type: "query", who: "workers" }])),
    // A Live View link gives the person who holds it the tab. all and end reach the browsers of each owner. settings is the limits.
    // These four are for the owner's own session only.
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.liveView": { type: "query", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.all": { type: "query", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.end": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.settings": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true }
  }
})
// %%
browser_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", browser_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced browser_service";
}
// %%
// Seconds unless the name says otherwise. The first three are the defaults of the settings the owner can change.
browserLimits = ({
  maxTabs: 8, // pages in one browser
  maxBrowsers: 10, // browsers up at one time, of all owners. Workers Paid has 200 for the account.
  maxPerOwner: 3, // browsers up at one time that one caller owns
  minSeconds: 10, // the least and the most one extend buys. The price reads the same two numbers.
  maxSeconds: 3600,
  runTimeout: 30000, // ms a run has when it names none
  keepAlive: 180000, // ms. Browser Run closes a browser that gets no command for this long: the stop if the tick does not run
  startWait: 15000, // ms a call waits for a browser that another call is starting
  startGrace: 30, // a start that has not finished after this long is taken to have died
  loadTimeout: 30000, // ms
  evalTimeout: 20000, // ms
  maxTimeout: 120000, // ms, eval and run
  evalBytes: 262144, // the JSON of an eval value
  textChars: 100000,
  shotBytes: 2000000,
  logEntries: 200,
  logChars: 500,
  includedSeconds: 36000, // 10 browser-hours a month are in the Workers Paid plan
  dollarsPerSecond: 0.000025 // $0.09 a browser-hour
})
// %%
// Why a URL is refused, or null. Only the address given is read: a redirect and what the page loads are not checked.
browserRefusal = (text) => {
  let u;
  try {
    u = new URL(String(text));
  } catch (e) {
    return "not a URL";
  }
  if (u.protocol !== "http:" && u.protocol !== "https:") return `${u.protocol} is not http or https`;
  const h = u.hostname.toLowerCase().replace(/^\[|\]$/g, "").replace(/\.$/, "");
  if (h === "localhost" || /\.(localhost|internal|local)$/.test(h)) return `${h} is a local name`;
  const v4 = h.match(/^(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)$/);
  if (v4) {
    const [a, b] = [Number(v4[1]), Number(v4[2])];
    if (a === 0 || a === 10 || a === 127 || (a === 169 && b === 254) || (a === 172 && b >= 16 && b <= 31) || (a === 192 && b === 168) || (a === 100 && b >= 64 && b <= 127))
      return `${h} is a private address`;
  }
